This is the coding agent's whole `Program.cs`, as the Getting started page lists it. It runs in the terminal.

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt].
// It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(),
    new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance,
        Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

The catalogue, `CodingCatalog`, belongs to the coding demo. It names the identities and packs that agent loads. The host lends the web access, telemetry and model providers. For an agent of your own, you write your own catalogue and identities. The page covers that in its build guides.

To run this one, set it up first:

```
cp demos/louis-agent/config/.env.example demos/louis-agent/config/.env
cp demos/louis-agent/config/.env.secrets.example demos/louis-agent/config/.secrets
```

Put `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in `.env.secrets`, or use a local model with `LLM_PROFILE=ollama`. Then, from `demos/louis-agent/hosts/LouisAgent.CodingCli`, run `dotnet run`. You need the .NET 10 SDK.

This comes from the Getting started page. The page doesn't give the path of its source doc.
